+\abstract{Strong-lensing time delays can test a proposed geometric response when the light preparation and lens-model degeneracies are controlled. We calculate a finite-body response in a fixed closed cosmological branch with a cubic scalar--tensor action. Compatible observer--lens, observer--source and lens--source maps are shared by the conventional and scalar calculations. The regular matter-sourced scalar produces a screened metric correction without a fitted local amplitude. For a body of \texorpdfstring{$10^{11}M_\odot$}{100 billion solar masses} and radius 3 kpc at redshift 0.3, with a source at redshift 1, the screening radius is approximately 212 kpc. The leading-order models differ by 25.96 s and 8.65 s in their two relative delays, 0.79--6.12 microarcsec in image positions, and 6.82 parts per million in the delay ratio. Adding the same finite-body second-order general-relativistic encounter correction to both changes these delay differences by less than 0.1 ms. Source, profile, mass-sheet and centering freedoms absorb the single-source difference. A positive conventional effective halo reproduces the complete leading scalar optical field. The results establish a reproducible conditional prediction and its equivalence limit, while scalar higher-order terms and global matching remain open.}
